<Project>

  <!--
    The coverage threshold and everything it may skip, in the tree rather than
    in a workflow input. A number passed on a command line is a number that can
    be lowered by whoever is standing at the console, and nothing would show it
    in a diff.

    Every route runs this. The properties sit in the project rather than in an
    argument, so `dotnet test` enforces the threshold in an editor, on a
    machine somebody types on, and in the workflow, and there is one number
    rather than one per route.

    THE THRESHOLD ONLY GOES UP. It is set to what the suite reaches now, not to
    a round number somebody would like. Raising it when the suite improves is
    the point; lowering it to make a red run green is the failure this file
    exists against, and a lowered number is a line in a diff with an author on
    it.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <CollectCoverage>true</CollectCoverage>
    <CoverletOutputFormat>cobertura</CoverletOutputFormat>
    <CoverletOutput>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)coverage/</CoverletOutput>

    <!--
      Line and branch together. A line threshold alone passes a suite that
      walks every line down one side of every condition, which is most of the
      way to testing nothing about the conditions.
    -->
    <ThresholdType>line,branch,method</ThresholdType>
    <ThresholdStat>total</ThresholdStat>

    <!--
      One number per type above, in that order, and each is what the suite
      actually reaches rounded down to a whole percent. Measured by running
      `dotnet test` in Release, which prints the table they come from.

      THE SAME SUITE MEASURES DIFFERENTLY ON THE TWO PLATFORMS IT RUNS ON, and
      the number here is the lower of them, because that is the one the gate
      that refuses will see:

          on the runner        | Jellyfin.Plugin.Stats | 4.34% | 100% | 20% |
          on a Windows clone   | Jellyfin.Plugin.Stats | 5.26% | 100% | 20% |

      One covered line out of twenty three, against one out of nineteen. The
      same source, so the two builds disagree about how many lines can be
      covered at all. Why they disagree is not established here, and a threshold
      taken from the higher one would have turned the runner red for a reason
      nobody changed. That already happened once, on the first push of this
      change.

      They are low because the assembly they are about is a plugin entry point
      and a configuration class the template left behind. That is what a
      percentage over a nearly empty assembly is worth, and it is stated here
      rather than dressed up: the number this issue really wants is the one the
      suite reaches once there is something to cover, and the milestones that
      add it are held behind the store question in #10.

      The rounding is down to the whole percent and no further. A round number
      well under the measurement is room to lose coverage without anything
      saying so.
    -->
    <Threshold>4,100,20</Threshold>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Coverage is measured over the plugin assembly. The suite is not the thing
      being covered, and the packages under it are somebody else's code, so
      including either would report a number about the wrong assembly. This is
      the scope of the measurement rather than an exclusion from it; the
      exclusions are the item group below.
    -->
    <Include>[Jellyfin.Plugin.Stats]*</Include>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      One line per exclusion, each naming a type and carrying its reason on the
      same line. A whole directory or a whole assembly is not allowed here: an
      exclusion that wide is a hole rather than an exclusion, and it hides
      everything that will later be added inside it.

      There are none today. The list exists so that the first one has to be
      argued for in a diff rather than added to a command line.
    -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ExcludeByAttribute>Obsolete,GeneratedCodeAttribute,CompilerGeneratedAttribute</ExcludeByAttribute>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
